## Install on macOS (Apple Silicon)

**With Homebrew (recommended):**

```sh
brew install --cask @TAP@/clipsync
```

The cask installs ClipSync to `/Applications`, puts `clipsync` on your PATH
@IF_UNSIGNED@and clears the download quarantine flag, so the app opens without a
Gatekeeper prompt.@END_UNSIGNED@@IF_SIGNED@and opens like any notarized app.@END_SIGNED@
`brew uninstall --cask clipsync` removes the app and keeps your settings, paired
devices and history. If you set up the background service, it stays registered:
remove it with `launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.clipsync` and delete
`~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.clipsync.plist`. `brew uninstall --zap --cask clipsync`
removes the service and all ClipSync data.

**From the disk image:**

1. Download `@DMG@` and open it.
2. Drag **ClipSync** onto **Applications**.
@IF_UNSIGNED@3. Open ClipSync from Applications. This build is not notarized by Apple, so
   macOS says it cannot verify that ClipSync is free of malware. Click **Done**.
4. Open **System Settings → Privacy & Security**, scroll down to **Security**,
   and click **Open Anyway** next to the message about ClipSync. Confirm with
   your password or Touch ID, then click **Open**. You only do this once.

Or, before the first launch, clear the quarantine flag in Terminal:

```sh
xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/ClipSync.app
```
@END_UNSIGNED@@IF_SIGNED@3. Open ClipSync from Applications. The app is signed with a Developer ID and
   notarized by Apple.
@END_SIGNED@
The command-line tool is inside the app at
`/Applications/ClipSync.app/Contents/MacOS/clipsync`.

## Install on Linux

```sh
tar -xzf clipsync-@VERSION@-linux-x86_64.tar.gz
cd clipsync-@VERSION@-linux-x86_64
./install-user.sh
```

@IF_AARCH64@Use the `aarch64` archive on 64-bit Arm. @END_AARCH64@`install-user.sh` copies `clipsync`
and `clipsync-desktop` to `~/.local/bin` and adds ClipSync to your app menu. It
needs no root and starts nothing; the app sets up its background service when
you finish setup. Instead of the terminal, you can open the extracted folder in
your file manager and run **ClipSync-Install.desktop**. Add `~/.local/bin` to
your PATH to use the `clipsync` command.

Requirements: glibc @GLIBC@ or newer and OpenSSL 3. The archives are built on
Ubuntu 22.04, so Ubuntu 22.04, Debian 12, Fedora 36 and current Arch Linux or
newer all qualify. The binaries link: @NEEDED@. The desktop app needs an X11 or
Wayland session; on X11 it also needs libxkbcommon-x11 (`libxkbcommon-x11-0` on
Debian and Ubuntu). Wayland clipboard sync needs a compositor that supports the
data-control protocol.

## Pair two computers

Install ClipSync on both computers and finish setup in the app or in
`clipsync tui`.

1. On the first computer, open **Devices** and choose **Allow a new device**
   (TUI: `2`, then `e`). A six-digit code appears.
2. On the second computer, open **Devices** and pick the first computer under
   **Nearby** (TUI: `f`, select it, `Enter`), or enter the address shown under
   the code (TUI: `m`). Then type the code.

Both computers then list each other under **Authorized devices**. Each code
works once, and nothing is saved until the code is verified. Pairing uses TCP
port 8485 and sync uses 8484 by default; ClipSync does not change firewall
rules.

## Check your download

In the folder that holds the download and `SHA256SUMS`:

```sh
shasum -a 256 -c SHA256SUMS --ignore-missing   # macOS
sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS --ignore-missing       # Linux
```

## Security

- ClipSync has not had an independent security or cryptographic review.
- Code pairing uses the `spake2` crate (RustCrypto 0.4), which has never had an
  independent audit. The key confirmation and transcript binding around it are
  ClipSync's own code, tested but not externally reviewed.
- Anyone who can see the code while it is on screen and connects first can
  pair, so only allow a device when you are about to type the code.
@IF_UNSIGNED@- The macOS app is not signed with an Apple Developer ID or notarized; it has
  only an ad-hoc signature. The Linux archives are not signed. Use `SHA256SUMS`
  to check what you downloaded.
@END_UNSIGNED@@IF_SIGNED@- The macOS app is signed with a Developer ID and notarized by Apple. The Linux
  archives are not signed; use `SHA256SUMS` to check them.
@END_SIGNED@
See [SECURITY.md](https://github.com/@REPO@/blob/@TAG@/docs/SECURITY.md) for
the full security model.

Built from @COMMIT@ with Rust @TOOLCHAIN@.
